---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';

const basic = `use dotcfg::{ConfigStack, DotCfg};

let defaults = DotCfg::new("myapp").at_dir("/etc/myapp");
let project = DotCfg::new("myapp").at_dir("./.myapp");

let cfg = ConfigStack::new()
    .layer("defaults", defaults)
    .layer("project", project)
    .write_to("project")
    .with_env_prefix("APP")
    .build()?;

let port: u16 = cfg.get_as("server.port")?;`;

const mergeExample = `# defaults
[server]
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 3000
tags = ["stable"]

# project
[server]
port = 8080
tags = ["local"]

# resolved
server.host = "127.0.0.1"
server.port = 8080
server.tags = ["local"]`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Layered configuration — dotcfg"
  description="Compose named DotCfg sources with explicit precedence, deep merging, environment overrides, and one write target."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">Core API</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Layered configuration
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      <code>DotCfg</code> represents one file-backed source. <code>ConfigStack</code> is the
      composition layer: it combines several named sources into one resolved view without changing
      who owns each value on disk.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">Build a stack</h2>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust" code={basic} />
    <p>
      Source order is precedence order. Earlier layers are lower priority; later layers override
      them. The stack-level environment prefix sits above every file layer.
    </p>

    <div class="my-[18px] flex items-center gap-3 rounded-[9px] border border-border bg-card p-4 max-[760px]:flex-col max-[760px]:items-start">
      <span class="font-bold">defaults</span><b class="text-primary">→</b>
      <span class="font-bold">project</span><b class="text-primary">→</b>
      <span class="font-bold">environment</span>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">Merge behavior</h2>
    <p>
      Nested maps deep-merge, so overriding one nested field does not erase its siblings. Arrays,
      scalars, and values with different shapes replace the lower-precedence value.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Resolution" code={mergeExample} />
    <p>
      Sources may use different file formats because each one is parsed into dotcfg's shared value
      tree before merging.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">Missing, empty, and malformed sources</h2>
    <p>
      A missing source file is skipped. A valid empty source contributes no values. A malformed
      source is an error; the stack does not silently ignore broken configuration and continue with
      lower-precedence layers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">Writes need an owner</h2>
    <p>
      Resolved reads can combine many sources, but writes go to exactly one source. Configure that
      source with <code>write_to()</code>. <code>set()</code>, <code>set_val()</code>, and{' '}
      <code>save()</code> then write only there.
    </p>
    <Callout type="warning" title="save() does not flatten the stack">
      <p>
        Saving through a stack writes the value you provide to the configured write target. dotcfg
        never serializes the merged resolved view back into that file, because doing so would copy
        values owned by other sources into the wrong source.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>
      Without <code>write_to()</code>, the stack is intentionally read-only and write methods return{' '}
      <code>Error::NoWriteTarget</code>. A duplicate source name returns{' '}
      <code>Error::DuplicateSource</code>; an unknown write target returns{' '}
      <code>Error::UnknownSource</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">Read one source directly</h2>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust" code={'let defaults = cfg.source("defaults")?;\nlet port: u16 = defaults.get_as("server.port")?;'} />
    <p>
      Direct source access bypasses stack resolution. This is useful when you need to inspect or
      edit one layer deliberately instead of asking for the effective value.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">Environment overrides</h2>
    <p>
      Stack environment variables use <code>__</code> for nesting while a single underscore remains
      part of a key: <code>APP_API_KEY</code> maps to <code>api_key</code>, and{' '}
      <code>APP_DATABASE__PORT</code> maps to <code>database.port</code>. The same mapping is used
      by <code>get()</code>, <code>get_as()</code>, and <code>load()</code>, including fields that
      exist only in the environment.
    </p>
    <Callout type="info" title="One stack prefix">
      <p>
        Resolved reads use the prefix configured on <code>ConfigStack</code>. Prefixes configured on
        individual <code>DotCfg</code> sources apply only when those sources are accessed directly.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">When to use ConfigStack</h2>
    <p>
      Use <code>DotCfg</code> when one file is the configuration source. Reach for{' '}
      <code>ConfigStack</code> when your application genuinely has several file-backed owners—for
      example system defaults plus project-local settings—and you need one effective read view with
      explicit precedence and persistence.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
